Yes, Sirius is well visible in Finland during winter, even in
Lapland. :> Fomalhaut, in Piscis Austrinus (the Southern Fish), has
even been seen (with a telescope) from the southern coast of Finland,
where it rises only a few arc minutes from the horizon.  I'm rather
positive that I saw Alphard, in Hydra, while I still lived in Lapland.
That star is more southernly than Sirius.

> Note that the stars are named after the constellation they're in. Within
> each constellation, they're ordered by magniture, so the brightest should
> be alfa, the second beta, etcetera. I cannot explain why this seems to be
> different in the constallation Orion, as you might have noted above. Also,
> in the southern hemishpere, in the constellation Raven, the order of
> magnitude is delta, gamma, beta, epsilon, alfa...

Sometimes the stars were given their Greek letter in order inside the
constellation.  The best example of this is Ursa Major, where the Big
Dipper stars are "lettered" in order Dubhe, Merak, Phecda, Megrez,
Alioth, Mizar and Alkaid.  Dubhe is the star that, with Merak, points
approximately to Polaris.  Moreover, this "order naming" is an older
way than the modern division into constellations, so the brightest
star isn't always called alpha.  I have no idea if either of these
explanations is applicable to Orion, though.

I got this information from _Tähtitaivas 2000_ by Karttunen, Manner &
Tuovinen.

